  "question": {
    "text": "The two lines $x=a y+b, z=c y+d ;$ and $x=a^{\\prime} y+b^{\\prime}, z=c^{\\prime} y+d^{\\prime}$ are perpendicular to each other if"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{aa}^{\\prime}+\\mathrm{cc}^{\\prime}=-1$\n",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{aa}^{\\prime}+\\mathrm{cc}^{\\prime}=1$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$\\frac{a}{a^{\\prime}}+\\frac{c}{c^{\\prime}}=-1$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$\\frac{a}{a^{\\prime}}+\\frac{c}{c^{\\prime}}=1$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Equation of lines $\\frac{x-b}{a}=y=\\frac{z-d}{c}$\n$\\frac{x-b^{\\prime}}{a^{\\prime}}=y=\\frac{z-d^{\\prime}}{c^{\\prime}}$\nLines are perpendicular $\\Rightarrow a a^{\\prime}+1+c c^{\\prime}=0$"
  }
}